## Where we are

::: {.incremental}
- You can now **fit** linear and logistic models and **check** them
- Today: turn output into **words and tables** a thesis committee or journal accepts
  - annotated walkthroughs of **linear** and **logistic** output
  - **results and methods** wording
  - **nine common pitfalls**
  - **what lies beyond** this workshop (models and packages)
:::

# Topic 9 — Reading the output {background-color="#2C3E50"}

## Linear regression: what each number means


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}


|Output           |Value       |How to say it                                                                              |
|:----------------|:-----------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|β (salt_g)       |1.80        |Each extra 1 g/day of salt ↔ this many mmHg higher mean SBP, other variables held constant |
|Std. error       |0.30        |Precision of β: smaller = more precise                                                     |
|95% CI           |1.2 to 2.4  |Plausible range for the true β — report it with every estimate                             |
|p-value          |< 0.001     |Chance of a β this far from 0 if the true β were 0 — not the size of the effect            |
|R² / adjusted R² |0.39 / 0.37 |Share of SBP variability the model accounts for — descriptive only                         |
|Residual SE      |13.5 mmHg   |The typical size of a prediction miss, in mmHg                                             |


:::
:::


## Logistic regression: what each number means


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}


|Output                      |Value        |How to say it                                                                   |
|:---------------------------|:------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|OR (salt_g)                 |1.24         |Each extra 1 g/day multiplies the ODDS of hypertension by this factor, adjusted |
|95% CI                      |1.11 to 1.39 |Plausible range for the OR; if it contains 1, no clear association              |
|p-value                     |< 0.001      |As for linear regression                                                        |
|Null → residual deviance    |687 → 511    |How much the covariates improve the likelihood-based fit                        |
|AIC                         |537          |Use to compare models fitted to the same rows (lower = better)                  |
|Pseudo-R² (McFadden / Tjur) |0.26 / 0.29  |Descriptive; not 'variance explained'; do not compare with linear R²            |
|AUC                         |0.82         |Discrimination: 0.5 = chance, 1 = perfect                                       |


:::
:::


## A publication-ready combined table


::: {.cell output-location='slide'}

```{.r .cell-code}
labs <- list(salt_g ~ "Salt intake (per 1 g/day)", age ~ "Age (per year)", bmi ~ "BMI (per kg/m²)",
             sex ~ "Sex", residence ~ "Residence", education ~ "Education", tobacco ~ "Tobacco use",
             alcohol ~ "Alcohol use", phys_act ~ "Physical activity")
t_lin <- tbl_regression(m_lin, label = labs) |> bold_labels()
t_log <- tbl_regression(m_log, exponentiate = TRUE, label = labs) |> bold_labels()
tbl_merge(list(t_lin, t_log), tab_spanner = c("**Systolic BP (β, mmHg)**", "**Hypertension (OR)**")) |>
  modify_header(estimate_1 ~ "**β**", estimate_2 ~ "**OR**")
```

